            <ArticleTitle>Outcomes in off-pump vs. on-pump coronary artery bypass grafting stratified by pre-operative risk profile: an assessment using propensity score.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2473-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the benefit of off-pump coronary surgery stratified by the pre-operative risk profile.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Prospective and multicentric cohort study. All consecutive patients undergoing a first coronary bypass procedure between November 2001 and November 2003 were potentially eligible. Pre-operative EuroSCORE and in-hospital outcomes were prospectively collected using strictly standardized criteria. To ensure optimal adjustment, a propensity score was constructed using clinically relevant variables and incorporating individual centres. Of 1602 patients who underwent a first coronary bypass, EuroSCORE could be calculated in 1585: 787 were of moderate/high pre-operative risk profile (EuroSCORE &gt; 3), of which 347 underwent off-pump procedures, and 798 were of low pre-operative risk profile (EuroSCORE &lt; or = 3), of which 349 underwent off-pump procedures. After risk adjusting for propensity score, off-pump patients had less major events (post-operative death, myocardial infarction, and need for reoperation). This benefit was higher in the low-risk stratum (OR ranged between 0.27 and 0.4; P = 0.02-0.07) than in the high-risk stratum (OR between 0.4 and 0.7; P, not significant).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In real-life conditions, off-pump coronary surgery may be more effective than on-pump surgery. In contrast with previous reports, our results suggest that this benefit may be higher in patients with low pre-operative risk.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
